from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "1. The Question, the Method and the Settings"),
("p", "Claim: the question of the apostolic and prophetic offices is answered by reading each passage in its own setting and asking three things of it: who is called an apostle or a prophet, by whom, and for what purpose. The words stand in the Gospels, in Acts, in Paul’s letters, in the letters of Peter and in the Revelation. They do not all name the same kind of person, and a reader who treats them as one office will miss what each passage says."),
("sub", "1.1 The question"),
("p", "The question is not whether God sends men. The Scriptures answer that at once: “How shall they preach, except they be sent?” (Romans 10:15). The question is narrower. The New Testament lists “first apostles, secondarily prophets” among the gifts God has set in the church (1 Corinthians 12:28), and the household of God is “built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets” (Ephesians 2:20). Are those offices still filled, and what do the passages mean by each word? These are questions about the persons and the offices, and they are the ones this volume answers."),
("sub", "1.2 The settings"),
("p", "The volume reads the offices in five settings, and each has its own chapters. The first is the word itself, in Greek and in the English of the King James Version (chapter 2). The second is the Twelve, chosen by the Lord and sent to the house of Israel (chapter 3). The third is the others called apostles in the New Testament (chapter 4). The fourth is Paul, with the signs of an apostle and the revelation committed to him (chapters 5 to 7). The fifth is the prophets, the foundation of Ephesians 2 and 3 and the gifts of the ascended Christ in Ephesians 4 (chapters 8 to 10)."),
("p", "The settings are not arranged to prove a conclusion. They are the order in which the text places them. The Twelve are named in the Gospels, before the cross. Paul is called in Acts 9, after it. The letters that speak of the foundation are Paul’s, and the book of Acts is the record that runs from the one to the other. The volume calls the period of Acts 9 to 28 the overlap, because Israel’s Prophecy Program was still operative while the Mystery Program had begun."),
("sub", "1.3 The method"),
("p", "The method has four parts. Each passage is quoted from the King James Version and checked word for word. Each is read in the chapter or the book in which it stands, with the verses around it. Each is asked who speaks, who hears and what purpose the passage names. And the volume says what a passage leaves unsaid, because an argument from silence is a different thing from an argument from a statement, and the reader is owed the difference."),
("p", "The volume rests its conclusion on three kinds of evidence. The first is what passages state: that the Lord chose twelve and named them apostles, that Paul calls himself “an apostle of Jesus Christ by the will of God,” that the mystery “is now revealed unto his holy apostles and prophets by the Spirit.” The second is what the record shows: that the last sign Acts records is on Malta, in the last chapter, and that no appointment of an apostle is recorded after Paul’s. The third is what the letters do not contain: no passage names a successor to an apostle or tells a church to appoint one. The first two are plain. The third is a silence, and the volume says so where it relies on it."),
("sub", "1.4 What the volume does not decide"),
("p", "The volume does not say whether Andronicus and Junia held the apostolic office, because Romans 16:7 can be read two ways in the English and the volume reports the wording as the King James Version gives it. It does not say what “that which is perfect” is, a question it leaves with Volume 2 of this series. It does not date the goal of Ephesians 4:13. And it makes no statement about God’s power, which the Scriptures leave with God."),
("tbl", "The settings and the passages read in each.", [
    ("Luke 6:13", T("Luke 6:13"), "The Lord chooses twelve from his disciples and names them apostles."),
    ("Acts 14:14", T("Acts 14:14"), "Barnabas is called an apostle beside Paul."),
    ("Galatians 1:1", T("Gal 1:1"), "Paul’s apostleship is not of men, neither by man."),
    ("Ephesians 2:20", T("Eph 2:20"), "The foundation of the apostles and prophets."),
    ("Ephesians 4:11", T("Eph 4:11"), "Apostles and prophets among the gifts of the ascended Christ."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The offices are read in five settings, in the order the text places them.",
    "Each passage is asked who is called, by whom and for what purpose.",
    "The volume says what each passage leaves unsaid.",
    "The volume decides nothing on Romans 16:7 and does not date Ephesians 4:13.",
  ]),
]
